"health_atlas_pineal_story_orient" = "Find the pineal gland";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_orient_caption" = "In this midline section, the pineal body sits behind the upper end of the third ventricle, just above the midbrain. The nearby pineal recess belongs to the ventricle. The body clock is farther forward, in the hypothalamus.";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_production" = "Zoom in: a pinealocyte makes melatonin";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_production_caption" = "Norepinephrine acts on the pinealocyte, promoting melatonin synthesis. Inside the cell, AANAT converts serotonin to N-acetylserotonin; ASMT then converts that intermediate to melatonin. Newly made melatonin passes out of the cell and into nearby blood vessels.";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_ref_aanat" = "Evidence: daily regulation of pineal AANAT in rats (Roseboom et al.)";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_ref_anatomy" = "Anatomy: the human pineal region (Fang and Meyers)";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_ref_light" = "Evidence: light and nighttime melatonin in humans (Lewy et al.)";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_ref_relay" = "Evidence: the autonomic relay to the pineal gland in rats (Larsen et al.)";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_ref_retina" = "Evidence: light-sensitive retinal ganglion cells that reach the clock (Berson et al.)";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_ref_rhythm" = "Evidence: the daily melatonin pattern in human blood (Brown et al.)";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_relay" = "Follow the nighttime nerve relay";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_relay_caption" = "Clock timing reaches the pineal gland indirectly: through the paraventricular nucleus, the upper thoracic spinal cord, and the superior cervical ganglion. During biological night, sympathetic endings in the gland release more norepinephrine.";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_retina" = "The retina gives the clock light information";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_retina_caption" = "Light reaches the retina at the back of the eye. Special retinal ganglion cells send signals to the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), the main circadian clock in the hypothalamus. Light helps set the clock; the clock keeps time even in darkness.";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_rhythm" = "Compare biological day and night";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_rhythm_caption" = "Circulating melatonin is generally lower during biological day. It rises during biological night and falls toward morning. Light reaching the eyes at night can suppress the rise; darkness alone does not set the timing.";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_scope" = "Normal pineal physiology, from the human brain to a single pinealocyte. Much of the evidence for the nerve relay and its cellular timing comes from mammals such as rats. The pathways and day–night curve are schematic, not measurements.";
"health_atlas_pineal_story_title" = "How the pineal gland signals night";
